|\n64\t| HLR-1 (validation campaign, go/no-go scope) | P0 | E001 |\n65\t| HLR-2 (supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime) | P0 | E002, E003 |\n66\t| HLR-3 (PyTorch ROCm training via Kueue, amd.com/gpu quota) | P1 | E004, E005 |\n67\t| HLR-4 (documentation of AMD support) | P2 | E006 |\n68\t\n69\tAll HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n70\t\n71\t## Component Mapping\n72\t\n73\tAll strategy-listed components (KServe, Serving Runtimes, Training Operator,\n74\tDistributed Workloads) are canonical names in `.context/rhai-components.txt`\n75\tand all have architecture context files — no new-component onboarding chain is\n76\tneeded. The ROCm vLLm image is a new container image in an existing midstream\n77\trepo (opendatahub-io/vllm), so per Rule 20 the serving side starts with the\n78\timage-build Implementation plus konflux-onboarding in parallel (no\n79\trepo-onboarding).\n80\t\n81\t- **KServe** is classified passive: no KServe code change is described — the\n82\t  InferenceService/ServingRuntime machinery is accelerator-agnostic and the\n83\t  change is the runtime image it deploys. Validation that InferenceServices\n84\t  schedule and serve on AMD Instinct nodes becomes an acceptance criterion on\n85\t  E002, validated by the same owning team (RHOAI Serving). No separate epic.\n86\t- **Documentation** (not in the strategy's affected components list, but\n87\t  required by HLR-4) is a canonical component; E006 is mapped to it.\n88\t\n89\t## Open Question Disposition\n90\t\n91\t| Open question | Disposition | Rationale |\n92\t|---------------|-------------|-----------|\n93\t| Hardware access (procurement / partner-lab unresolved) | Precondition AC on E001 (and hardware-dependent validation ACs on E004/E005), plus health warning | The answer does not change which epics exist or what they do — the campaign epic exists either way and is blocked, not restructured, if hardware is unavailable. Procurement is a human/organizational process, not technical investigation work. |\n94\t| Upstream ROCm build cadence vs release trains | AC on E002 (pin image digest from an upstream release aligned with the platform release train; track cadence) | Informational: the productization epic builds a ROCm runtime regardless of cadence; cadence affects timing and pinning, not epic structure. Not a true Investigation (Rule 4). |\n95\t| Which models clear validation (implicit in HLR-1) | Investigation E001 gating E002/E003 | The answer changes downstream scope and existence — the defining trait of a true Investigation. The outcome space is structurally bounded (go with a cleared-model subset → productize that subset; no-go → remove productization), so it is modeled as a gate with `gate_failure_impact` rather than separate branch files: the \"no-go\" branch contains zero epics, and the \"go\" branch is the main-plan E002/E003 with model-set scope resolved by the gate. |\n96\t\n97\t## Health Warnings\n98\t\n99\t- **Missing strategy sections**: no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies\n100\t  table, no formal Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional\n101\t  Requirements section. Teams were taken from architecture context owning-team\n102\t  fields; epic acceptance criteria were derived from HLR text.\n103\t- **P0 blocked on non-engineering precondition**: HLR-1 (P0) cannot start\n104\t  until AMD Instinct hardware access is secured (procurement or partner lab).\n105\t  This is outside engineering control and is the schedule risk for the whole\n106\t  serving thread.\n107\t- **Docs priority appears inverted vs HLR-4**: HLR-4 is P2, but per the\n108\t  docs-authoring priority exception the docs epic derives P1 from the strategy\n109\t  frontmatter priority (Major → P1). Flagged for human verification.\n110\t- **Documentation component owner unknown**: neither the strategy nor\n111\t  architecture context names an owning team for the Documentation component;\n112\t  \"RHOAI Docs\" is assumed and should be corrected at triage if wrong.\n113\t- **Parity thresholds undefined**: the strategy does not state quantitative\n114\t  functional/performance parity thresholds for the go/no-go decision. Defining\n115\t  them is included in E001 scope (an implementation detail for the campaign\n116\t  team, not a structural unknown — the campaign epic exists either way).\n117\t\n118\t## Tiered Delivery\n119\t\n120\t- **Tier 1** (independent value / risk reduction): E001 (validation campaign —\n121\t  the strategy's core risk-reduction deliverable), E004 and E005 (PyTorch ROCm\n122\t  training and quota handling deliver user-facing value independent of the\n123\t  serving campaign outcome).\n124\t- **Tier 2** (gated on campaign outcome): E002, E003 (serving productization\n125\t  and its build pipeline, gated by E001). E006 (docs) follows all\n126\t  implementations.\n127\t\n128\tThe external dependency (AMD-controlled upstream ROCm image cadence) has\n129\tuncertain timing but does not gate Tier 1 work; it is handled as an AC/risk on\n130\tE002 rather than an upstream-PR epic, because the strategy describes consuming\n131\tAMD/community-published images, not submitting changes upstream.\n132\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f590fb60777572e","agent_id":"a913619b5c3699182","session_id":"b78dd159-7f4d-4694-995d-eb214297feef","uuid":"f92b636a-525b-428d-955b-5fe76d76061c","timestamp":"2026-10-21T17:43:22.863Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-be8d06e76065b7d3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
