{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b86837423fbf3a77","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020\n11\t\n12\tAMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training.\n13\t\n14\t## Triage\n15\t\n16\tNot below-threshold: the strategy spans four components and two owning teams,\n17\tand contains a genuine unknown (the HLR-1 validation campaign's go/no-go\n18\toutcome determines the scope and existence of the HLR-2 productization work).\n19\tNot docs-only: code, image, and pipeline changes are required.\n20\t\n21\t## Epic List\n22\t\n23\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n24\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM serving parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Konflux build pipeline for the vLLM ROCm serving image | Implementation (konflux-onboarding) | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | PyTorch ROCm training support for Training Operator jobs | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Kueue quota handling for amd.com/gpu resources | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E006 | Document AMD GPU (ROCm) support | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | P1 |\n31\t\n32\t## Dependency DAG\n33\t\n34\t```mermaid\n35\tgraph TD\n36\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 Validation campaign]\n37\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 vLLM ROCm runtime productization]\n38\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 Konflux ROCm image pipeline]\n39\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 PyTorch ROCm training]\n40\t    E005[RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota]\n41\t    E006[RHAISTRAT-9020-E006 Documentation]\n42\t    E001 --> E002\n43\t    E001 --> E003\n44\t    E002 --> E006\n45\t    E003 --> E006\n46\t    E004 --> E006\n47\t    E005 --> E006\n48\t```\n49\t\n50\t## DAG Justification\n51\t\n52\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n53\t|------|------|-----------|\n54\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates affected Implementations) | The campaign's go/no-go outcome and cleared-model list determine whether and for which models the ROCm serving runtime is productized. True gate: `rewrite` (scope = validated model set); a full no-go removes the epic. Recorded via `gated_by` and `gate_failure_impact` on E002. |\n55\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 | If the campaign no-goes, no ROCm image is productized and the dedicated build pipeline is moot; epic existence depends on the campaign outcome. True gate: `remove` on full no-go. |\n56\t| E002 ∥ E003 (no edge) | Rule 10 | Konflux onboarding config is independent of the runtime code/image work; coupling is enforced by the \"build pipeline green\" AC on E002 (Rule 25), never by a DAG edge. |\n57\t| E004 (root) | Rule 14 | Training work lives in a different repo/component from serving; no shared artifacts with E001–E003. HLR-3 is not gated by the serving-focused campaign (HLR-1 scopes vLLM serving only), so it delivers independent partial value. |\n58\t| E005 (root) | Rules 14/15 | Kueue quota configuration is a separate component from the Training Operator; the amd.com/gpu quota config is a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact, so E004 needs no edge — the end-to-end admission check is an AC on E004. |\n59\t| E002 → E006, E003 → E006, E004 → E006, E005 → E006 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring is blocked by all Implementation epics; docs describe what was built (validated model list, pipeline, training support). Edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n60\t\n61\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n62\t\n63\t| HLR | Priority | Epics |\n64\t|-----|----------|-------|\n65\t| HLR-1 (validation campaign, go/no-go scope) | P0 | E001 |\n66\t| HLR-2 (supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime) | P0 | E002, E003 |\n67\t| HLR-3 (PyTorch ROCm training via Kueue, amd.com/gpu quota) | P1 | E004, E005 |\n68\t| HLR-4 (documentation of AMD support) | P2 | E006 |\n69\t\n70\tAll HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n71\t\n72\t## Component Mapping\n73\t\n74\tAll strategy-listed components (KServe, Serving Runtimes, Training Operator,\n75\tDistributed Workloads) are canonical names in `.context/rhai-components.txt`\n76\tand all have architecture context files — no new-component onboarding chain is\n77\tneeded. The ROCm vLLm image is a new container image in an existing midstream\n78\trepo (opendatahub-io/vllm), so per Rule 20 the serving side starts with the\n79\timage-build Implementation plus konflux-onboarding in parallel (no\n80\trepo-onboarding).\n81\t\n82\t- **KServe** is classified passive: no KServe code change is described — the\n83\t  InferenceService/ServingRuntime machinery is accelerator-agnostic and the\n84\t  change is the runtime image it deploys. Validation that InferenceServices\n85\t  schedule and serve on AMD Instinct nodes becomes an acceptance criterion on\n86\t  E002, validated by the same owning team (RHOAI Serving). No separate epic.\n87\t- **Documentation** (not in the strategy's affected components list, but\n88\t  required by HLR-4) is a canonical component; E006 is mapped to it.\n89\t\n90\t## Open Question Disposition\n91\t\n92\t| Open question | Disposition | Rationale |\n93\t|---------------|-------------|-----------|\n94\t| Hardware access (procurement / partner-lab unresolved) | Precondition AC on E001 (and hardware-dependent validation ACs on E004/E005), plus health warning | The answer does not change which epics exist or what they do — the campaign epic exists either way and is blocked, not restructured, if hardware is unavailable. Procurement is a human/organizational process, not technical investigation work. |\n95\t| Upstream ROCm build cadence vs release trains | AC on E002 (pin image digest from an upstream release aligned with the platform release train; track cadence) | Informational: the productization epic builds a ROCm runtime regardless of cadence; cadence affects timing and pinning, not epic structure. Not a true Investigation (Rule 4). |\n96\t| Which models clear validation (implicit in HLR-1) | Investigation E001 gating E002/E003 | The answer changes downstream scope and existence — the defining trait of a true Investigation. The outcome space is structurally bounded (go with a cleared-model subset → productize that subset; no-go → remove productization), so it is modeled as a gate with `gate_failure_impact` rather than separate branch files: the \"no-go\" branch contains zero epics, and the \"go\" branch is the main-plan E002/E003 with model-set scope resolved by the gate. |\n97\t\n98\t## Health Warnings\n99\t\n100\t- **Missing strategy sections**: no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies\n101\t  table, no formal Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional\n102\t  Requirements section. Teams were taken from architecture context owning-team\n103\t  fields; epic acceptance criteria were derived from HLR text.\n104\t- **P0 blocked on non-engineering precondition**: HLR-1 (P0) cannot start\n105\t  until AMD Instinct hardware access is secured (procurement or partner lab).\n106\t  This is outside engineering control and is the schedule risk for the whole\n107\t  serving thread.\n108\t- **Docs priority appears inverted vs HLR-4**: HLR-4 is P2, but per the\n109\t  docs-authoring priority exception the docs epic derives P1 from the strategy\n110\t  frontmatter priority (Major → P1). Flagged for human verification.\n111\t- **Documentation component owner unknown**: neither the strategy nor\n112\t  architecture context names an owning team for the Documentation component;\n113\t  \"RHOAI Docs\" is assumed and should be corrected at triage if wrong.\n114\t- **Parity thresholds undefined**: the strategy does not state quantitative\n115\t  functional/performance parity thresholds for the go/no-go decision. Defining\n116\t  them is included in E001 scope (an implementation detail for the campaign\n117\t  team, not a structural unknown — the campaign epic exists either way).\n118\t\n119\t## Tiered Delivery\n120\t\n121\t- **Tier 1** (independent value / risk reduction): E001 (validation campaign —\n122\t  the strategy's core risk-reduction deliverable), E004 and E005 (PyTorch ROCm\n123\t  training and quota handling deliver user-facing value independent of the\n124\t  serving campaign outcome).\n125\t- **Tier 2** (gated on campaign outcome): E002, E003 (serving productization\n126\t  and its build pipeline, gated by E001). E006 (docs) follows all\n127\t  implementations.\n128\t\n129\tThe external dependency (AMD-controlled upstream ROCm image cadence) has\n130\tuncertain timing but does not gate Tier 1 work; it is handled as an AC/risk on\n131\tE002 rather than an upstream-PR epic, because the strategy describes consuming\n132\tAMD/community-published images, not submitting changes upstream.\n133\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aaa2c4ed914d3629","agent_id":"af547be916899f51a","session_id":"b78dd159-7f4d-4694-995d-eb214297feef","uuid":"6228a212-3384-40c6-992c-3b364c734390","timestamp":"2026-10-21T17:45:35.351Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b86837423fbf3a77","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9020"}
